sequenceDiagram
  %% status: draft
  %% command: proof:run
  %% kind: proof.run
  autonumber
  participant CLI as CI job / CLI
  participant GW as Gateway
  participant GH as GitHub
  participant Q as Proof queue<br/>FIFO
  participant SW as Swamp
  participant VM as Proof VMs
  CLI->>GW: Record the proof
  %%> Record commit, authenticated caller and options, then queue admission. Only an active authenticated ci-runner Node uses the CI pool. Operator proofs join task admission.
  GW-->>CLI: Follow one proof Operation
  GW->>GH: Select commands<br/>check trust
  %%> Read the trusted commit, selection list and cases. A fork needs approval of this head and run-proofs. No selected command succeeds without a VM.
  CLI->>CLI: Expand base and head<br/>with the runner
  %%> The runner validates and expands fixtures, list vars, variants and batch setup. Compare its canonical per-case definitions by id. Command code changes select the whole file. Runner, selection code or selection list changes disable narrowing for every selected command. Tooling alone selects no commands. Unknown fixture keys and any planning doubt select the whole file.
  CLI->>GH: Publish the selection<br/>for this base and head
  %%> CI consumes this same result and uploads proof-selection.json before proof admission. Include every considered file, even when it selects zero cases.
  GW->>GH: Read the runner selection<br/>for this base and head
  %%> Accept only the exact version, head, merge base and cases file. Missing, expired, malformed or mismatched results select the whole file. The Gateway does not compare YAML definitions.
  GW->>GW: Record case ids<br/>and reasons
  %%> Select added and changed cases and their variants, and users whose expanded dependencies changed. Deleted cases select nothing. Report changed, added, uses changed fixture, uses changed var, batch setup changed, command code changed or proof tooling changed. A verified zero-case selection succeeds without a VM.
  GW->>GW: Plan selected cases<br/>in batches
  %%> Expand selected variant ids only. Sum measured case seconds from prior reports; unseen cases use the measured median, or one second with no history.
  GW->>Q: Join by proof Operation id
  %%> When:
  %%> - Earlier open parents without runs block both operator and CI proofs, including during selection. Ended or cancelled parents and proofs with nothing to prove leave the queue. An older parent's own 7200-second admission allowance bounds its wait.
  Q->>GW: Admit the front proof
  %%> Why:
  %%> - The Swamp Node shares one CPU core, so only one proof works at a time. Under the admission transaction, check the place before creating groups or children; record runs and the execution start time together.
  %%> When:
  %%> - An open parent with runs or an unresolved child holds the place. Stale children and their parent's run list cannot hold it alone. Parent cancellation alone cannot free it.
  %%> - A held start stays protected while its parent is open within its 7200-second allowance from creation. The watchdog cancels unreleased children without a native run when their parent ended or is missing, using normal group cleanup.
  %%> - Other parents retry every 20 seconds. While a proof holds the place, show "Waiting for proof Operation <id> to finish." as the step and capacity reason. A waiting parent reads `waiting` and has no start time; at admission it reads `running` with its start time, and the step clears. It holds no place.
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - A child that nothing ends stops holding the place after its own deadline plus the existing 60-minute stale-hold bound. Without a deadline, use its last activity plus the longest shard budget and that bound. A timed-out lock holder uses its finish time, or last activity when absent, plus the bound.
  %%> - If that cancellation is lost, a held child of an ended or missing parent stops holding the place 60 minutes after its last activity. Admission past a stale child warns after commit and gateway:status reports gateway.proof_place_stale_child. Admission never changes the stale child.
  %%> - Waiting more than 7200 seconds from creation fails proof.capacity_timeout, for both operator and CI proofs. A full VM pool or stale base uses the same allowance and its existing VM wait step.
  GW->>GW: Balance whole batches<br/>by measured time
  %%> Longest measured batch first; assign each to the least loaded VM. Selected cases of a batch stay together. Record expected cases and all shard operations atomically. Each shard records its run budget: 600 s for the shard, 180 s for each batch and the measured case seconds, times 1.5, rounded up to five minutes, at most two hours.
  GW->>GW: Hold admitted child starts
  %%> Keep unrelated Operations able to start while this proof hands over its children. Execution copies must all succeed before the first start is queued.
  loop each admitted child, in order
    GW->>SW: Start one run per VM
    %%> Queue only the first start, then queue its successor 20 seconds after its answer, including refusal or definite job failure. A lost confirmation never permits resubmission. Its successor waits until recovery binds the run or the child ends, bounded by the released child's 600-second admission deadline. The next start is due 20 seconds after that resolution. Before submission, an ended or cancelled parent cancels the child through normal cancellation and group cleanup.
    %%> A held child has no native admission deadline. Release starts its normal 600-second admission budget and preserves its captured execution budget. A lost successor uses the existing watchdog: after the parent's allowance, all children quiet for 300 seconds makes unstarted children fail with proof.admission_interrupted and cleans up their groups.
  end
  SW->>VM: Claim a place
  %%> Fresh VM from the host base at the trusted commit. No CI keep-failed option.
  SW->>VM: Read the workspace commit
  SW->>SW: Check the workspace commit
  loop each assigned batch, in order
    SW->>VM: Restore the head checkout
    %%> A prior flagged-case proof may have switched to the merge base. Restore the admitted head before planning the next batch.
    SW->>VM: Read the command batches
    SW->>SW: Select the assigned batch
    SW->>VM: Restore the base snapshot
    SW->>VM: Run batch setup
    SW->>VM: Run selected cases
    opt CI replay after every head case passed
      SW->>SW: Check head verdicts
      SW->>VM: Check out the base
      SW->>VM: Select replay cases
      %%> Use the runner's case-level comparison, then keep fails_on_base: true. Unchanged flags and unflagged cases never replay, even under whole-file code or tooling selection.
      SW->>VM: Remove the base copy
      SW->>SW: Scope replay case ids
      %%> Keep only cases admitted to this batch.
      SW->>VM: Switch to the base
      SW->>VM: Read nested commit
      SW->>SW: Check nested commit
      SW->>VM: Take head case files
      %%> Copy cases, batches and the spec loader. Keep base runner and step implementations.
      SW->>VM: Restore base snapshot
      SW->>VM: Run replay setup
      SW->>VM: Run replay cases
      SW->>SW: Join replay verdicts
      %%> Each replay must fail with cause assertion. A pass, another cause or missing evidence fails the proof with a CASE@merge-base verdict. Keep flags after merge; only a later case change opts them into another replay.
    end
    VM-->>SW: Verdict lines
    %%> The Gateway records the verdict lines of each batch on its shard as the batch ends. A shard that ends at its run budget keeps them; only the cases of the batches that did not end are unrun.
  end
  SW->>VM: Release the place
  %%> Delete after every outcome. Failed or missing cleanup schedules normal group cancellation. A live or releasing VM holds its place until confirmed deletion.
  SW-->>GW: Report verdicts<br/>full artifact
  %%> Capture the full report, not its 4 KiB history tail. Identify command and file on each line. Missing, duplicate or unexpected case verdicts prevent a pass; a missing expected case is unrun.
  GW->>GW: Merge all shard verdicts
  %%> Wait for every admitted child, require every expected case and every selected command, and close one proof result. Preserve a native interrupted shard as inconclusive interrupted coverage, even with all passed rows. A completed failed case or a non-interrupted child that did not pass takes precedence and fails the proof. Keep every case verdict, including synthesized unrun rows. Cancellation and admission interruption keep their existing failure semantics.
  GW-->>CLI: One merged report
  CLI->>CLI: Require the proof verdict
  %%> The proof job is in Required checks. A selected command with a failed case or no verdict fails. An interrupted result stays interrupted in the receipt and exits nonzero, including with all passed rows. Malformed coverage or a nonzero CLI exit never passes. No selected command passes. Capacity is only a bounded wait, never a successful no-case result.
  %%> A fork uses the outside proof's status on this exact head. A missing, pending or failed status fails Required checks; rerun the failed Required job after the approved outside proof succeeds.
